data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"## What a derivative measures\n\nStart from the definition, read as a statement about nudges:\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(a+\\Delta x) - f(a)}{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nIn words: move the input slightly away from $a$, look at how far the output moves, and divide. As the nudge gets smaller, that ratio settles on one number, and that number is $f'(a)$. Rearranged, the definition says:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> For a small nudge $\\Delta x$ at the point $a$: $\\quad \\Delta f \\approx f'(a)\\,\\Delta x$.\n> The derivative is the **stretch factor** at $a$. It's the number an input nudge gets multiplied by to become the output nudge.\n\nTwo things follow from reading it this way, and both matter later:\n\n- **The stretch factor depends on where you are.** $f(x) = x^2$ stretches nudges by $6$ at $x=3$ and by $1$ at $x = 0.5$. \"The derivative of $f$\" isn't one number. It's a different stretch factor at each input.\n- **The sign tells you the direction.** A stretch factor of $-2$ doubles the nudge and also reverses it. If the input moves right, the output moves left.\n\nThe $\\approx$ means the error shrinks faster than $\\Delta x$ does. Make the nudge 10 times smaller and the error gets much more than 10 times smaller. That's why \"multiply by the stretch factor\" becomes exact in the limit.\n\nThis is the first of the three foundations. Before we stack anything on it, I want to check that the \"stretch factor at a point\" reading feels obvious rather than just familiar. Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field, even if it's one line."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01Xss3nLMjpoEo4ZsrA5Nehs","name":"quiz","arguments":{"type":"check"},"partialJson":"{\"type\": \"check"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":17510,"cache