ils":"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to"},"partialJson":"{\"type\": \"check\", \"nodeId\": \"def-derivative\", \"question\": \"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?\", \"details\": \"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":17510,"cacheWrite":16029,"totalTokens":33547,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":16029},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860994219,"responseId":"msg_011CgMR1Z5HBq5VkbdJ6CTE6"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"## What a derivative measures\n\nStart from the definition, read as a statement about nudges:\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(a+\\Delta x) - f(a)}{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nIn words: move the input slightly away from $a$, look at how far the output moves, and divide. As the nudge gets smaller, that ratio settles on one number, and that number is $f'(a)$. Rearranged, the definition says:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> For a small nudge $\\Delta x$ at the point $a$: $\\quad \\Delta f \\approx f'(a)\\,\\Delta x$.\n> The derivative is the **stretch factor** at $a$. It's the number an input nudge gets multiplied by to become the output nudge.\n\nTwo things follow from reading it this way, and both matter later:\n\n- **The stretch factor depends on where you are.** $f(x) = x^2$ stretches nudges by $6$ at $x=3$ and by $1$ at $x = 0.5$. \"The derivative of $f$\" isn't one number. It's a different stretch factor at each input.\n- **The sign tells you the direction.** A stretch factor of $-2$ doubles the nudge and also reverses it. If the input moves right, the output moves left.\n\nThe $\\approx$ means the error shrinks faster than $\\Delta x$ does. Make the nudge 10 times smaller and the error gets much more than 10 times smaller. That's why \"multiply by the stretch factor\" becomes exact in the limit.\n\nThis is the first of the three foundations. Before we stack anything on it, I want to check that the \"stretch factor at a point\" reading feels obvious rather than just familiar. Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field, even if it's one line."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01Xss3nLMjpoEo4ZsrA5Nehs","name":"quiz","arguments":{"type":"check","nodeId":"def-derivative","question":"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?","details":"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge."},"partialJson":"{\"type\": \"check\", \"nodeId\": \"def-derivative\", \"question\": \"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?\", \"details\": \"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge."}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":17510,"cacheWrite":16029,"totalTokens":33547,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":16029},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860994219,"responseId":"msg_011CgMR1Z5HBq5VkbdJ6CTE6"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"## What a derivative measures\n\nStart from the definition, read as a statement about nudges:\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(a+\\Delta x) - f(a)}{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nIn words: move the input slightly away from $a$, look at how far the output moves, and divide. As the nudge gets smaller, that ratio settles on one number, and that number is $f'(a)$. Rearranged, the definition says:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> For a small nudge $\\Delta x$ at the point $a$: $\\quad \\Delta f \\approx f'(a)\\,\\Delta x$.\n> The derivative is the **stretch factor** at $a$. It's the number an input nudge gets multiplied by to become the output nudge.\n\nTwo things follow from reading it this way, and both matter later:\n\n- **The stretch factor depends on where you are.** $f(x) = x^2$ stretches nudges by $6$ at $x=3$ and by $1$ at $x = 0.5$. \"The derivative of $f$\" isn't one number. It's a different stretch factor at each input.\n- **The sign tells you the direction.** A stretch factor of $-2$ doubles the nudge and also reverses it. If the input moves right, the output moves left.\n\nThe $\\approx$ means the error shrinks faster than $\\Delta x$ does. Make the nudge 10 times smaller and the error gets much more than 10 times smaller. That's why \"multiply by the stretch factor\" becomes exact in the limit.\n\nThis is the first of the three foundations. Before we stack anything on it, I want to check that the \"stretch factor at a point\" reading feels obvious rather than just familiar. Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field, even if it's one line."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01Xss3nLMjpoEo4ZsrA5Nehs","name":"quiz","arguments":{"type":"check","nodeId":"def-derivative","question":"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?","details":"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge.","options":[{"label":"$+0.02$","value":"pos"},{"label":"$-0.02$","value":"neg"},{"label":"$+0.0001$","value":"sq"},{"label":"$-0.01$","value":"nostretch"}],"explanation":"The stretch factor at $x=-1$ is $f'(-1) = 2\\cdot(-1) = -2$. The nudge gets doubled and reversed:\n$$\\Delta f \\approx f'(-1)\\,\\"},"partialJson":"{\"type\": \"check\", \"nodeId\": \"def-derivative\", \"question\": \"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?\", \"details\": \"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge.\", \"options\": [{\"label\": \"$+0.02$\", \"value\": \"pos\"}, {\"label\": \"$-0.02$\", \"value\": \"neg\"}, {\"label\": \"$+0.0001$\", \"value\": \"sq\"}, {\"label\": \"$-0.01$\", \"value\": \"nostretch\"}], \"explanation\": \"The stretch factor at $x=-1$ is $f'(-1) = 2\\\\cdot(-1) = -2$. The nudge gets doubled and reversed:\\n$$\\\\Delta f \\\\approx f'(-1)\\\\,\\\\"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":17510,"cacheWrite":16029,"totalTokens":33547,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":16029},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860994219,"responseId":"msg_011CgMR1Z5HBq5VkbdJ6CTE6"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"## What a derivative measures\n\nStart from the definition, read as a statement about nudges:\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(a+\\Delta x) - f(a)}{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nIn words: move the input slightly away from $a$, look at how far the output moves, and divide. As the nudge gets smaller, that ratio settles on one number, and that number is $f'(a)$. Rearranged, the definition says:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> For a small nudge $\\Delta x$ at the point $a$: $\\quad \\Delta f \\approx f'(a)\\,\\Delta x$.\n> The derivative is the **stretch factor** at $a$. It's the number an input nudge gets multiplied by to become the output nudge.\n\nTwo things follow from reading it this way, and both matter later:\n\n- **The stretch factor depends on where you are.** $f(x) = x^2$ stretches nudges by $6$ at $x=3$ and by $1$ at $x = 0.5$. \"The derivative of $f$\" isn't one number. It's a different stretch factor at each input.\n- **The sign tells you the direction.** A stretch factor of $-2$ doubles the nudge and also reverses it. If the input moves right, the output moves left.\n\nThe $\\approx$ means the error shrinks faster than $\\Delta x$ does. Make the nudge 10 times smaller and the error gets much more than 10 times smaller. That's why \"multiply by the stretch factor\" becomes exact in the limit.\n\nThis is the first of the three foundations. Before we stack anything on it, I want to check that the \"stretch factor at a point\" reading feels obvious rather than just familiar. Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field, even if it's one line."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01Xss3nLMjpoEo4ZsrA5Nehs","name":"quiz","arguments":{"type":"check","nodeId":"def-derivative","question":"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?","details":"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge.","options":[{"label":"$+0.02$","value":"pos"},{"label":"$-0.02$","value":"neg"},{"label":"$+0.0001$","value":"sq"},{"label":"$-0.01$","value":"nostretch"}],"explanation":"The stretch factor at $x=-1$ is $f'(-1) = 2\\cdot(-1) = -2$. The nudge gets doubled and reversed:\n$$\\Delta f \\approx f'(-1)\\,\\Delta x = -2 \\times 0.01 = -0.02$$\nYou can check it directly: $(-0.99)^2 = 0.9801$, which is $0.0199$ less than $1$, so about $-0.02$.\n\n- $+0.02$ uses the right size but"},"partialJson":"{\"type\": \"check\", \"nodeId\": \"def-derivative\", \"question\": \"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?\", \"details\": \"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge.\", \"options\": [{\"label\": \"$+0.02$\", \"value\": \"pos\"}, {\"label\": \"$-0.02$\", \"value\": \"neg\"}, {\"label\": \"$+0.0001$\", \"value\": \"sq\"}, {\"label\": \"$-0.01$\", \"value\": \"nostretch\"}], \"explanation\": \"The stretch factor at $x=-1$ is $f'(-1) = 2\\\\cdot(-1) = -2$. The nudge gets doubled and reversed:\\n$$\\\\Delta f \\\\approx f'(-1)\\\\,\\\\Delta x = -2 \\\\times 0.01 = -0.02$$\\nYou can check it directly: $(-0.99)^2 = 0.9801$, which is $0.0199$ less than $1$, so about $-0.02$.\\n\\n- $+0.02$ uses the right size but"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":17510,"cacheWrite":16029,"totalTokens":33547,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":16029},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860994219,"responseId":"msg_011CgMR1Z5HBq5VkbdJ6CTE6"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"## What a derivative measures\n\nStart from the definition, read as a statement about nudges:\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(a+\\Delta x) - f(a)}{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nIn words: move the input slightly away from $a$, look at how far the output moves, and divide. As the nudge gets smaller, that ratio settles on one number, and that number is $f'(a)$. Rearranged, the definition says:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> For a small nudge $\\Delta x$ at the point $a$: $\\quad \\Delta f \\approx f'(a)\\,\\Delta x$.\n> The derivative is the **stretch factor** at $a$. It's the number an input nudge gets multiplied by to become the output nudge.\n\nTwo things follow from reading it this way, and both matter later:\n\n- **The stretch factor depends on where you are.** $f(x) = x^2$ stretches nudges by $6$ at $x=3$ and by $1$ at $x = 0.5$. \"The derivative of $f$\" isn't one number. It's a different stretch factor at each input.\n- **The sign tells you the direction.** A stretch factor of $-2$ doubles the nudge and also reverses it. If the input moves right, the output moves left.\n\nThe $\\approx$ means the error shrinks faster than $\\Delta x$ does. Make the nudge 10 times smaller and the error gets much more than 10 times smaller. That's why \"multiply by the stretch factor\" becomes exact in the limit.\n\nThis is the first of the three foundations. Before we stack anything on it, I want to check that the \"stretch factor at a point\" reading feels obvious rather than just familiar. Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field, even if it's one line."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01Xss3nLMjpoEo4ZsrA5Nehs","name":"quiz","arguments":{"type":"check","nodeId":"def-derivative","question":"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?","details":"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge.","options":[{"label":"$+0.02$","value":"pos"},{"label":"$-0.02$","value":"neg"},{"label":"$+0.0001$","value":"sq"},{"label":"$-0.01$","value":"nostretch"}],"explanation":"The stretch factor at $x=-1$ is $f'(-1) = 2\\cdot(-1) = -2$. The nudge gets doubled and reversed:\n$$\\Delta f \\approx f'(-1)\\,\\Delta x = -2 \\times 0.01 = -0.02$$\nYou can check it directly: $(-0.99)^2 = 0.9801$, which is $0.0199$ less than $1$, so about $-0.02$.\n\n- $+0.02$ uses the right size but the stretch factor from $x = +1$ instead of $x=-1$. The stretch factor is local to where the input sits.\n- $+0.0001$ squares the nudge. That gives the value of $f(\\Delta x)$, not the change in $f$ near $-1$.\n- $-0.01$ gets the direction right but leaves out the doubling.","correctAnswer":"neg"},"partialJson":"{\"type\": \"check\", \"nodeId\": \"def-derivative\", \"question\": \"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?\", \"details\": \"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge.\", \"options\": [{\"label\": \"$+0.02$\", \"value\": \"pos\"}, {\"label\": \"$-0.02$\", \"value\": \"neg\"}, {\"label\": \"$+0.0001$\", \"value\": \"sq\"}, {\"label\": \"$-0.01$\", \"value\": \"nostretch\"}], \"explanation\": \"The stretch factor at $x=-1$ is $f'(-1) = 2\\\\cdot(-1) = -2$. The nudge gets doubled and reversed:\\n$$\\\\Delta f \\\\approx f'(-1)\\\\,\\\\Delta x = -2 \\\\times 0.01 = -0.02$$\\nYou can check it directly: $(-0.99)^2 = 0.9801$, which is $0.0199$ less than $1$, so about $-0.02$.\\n\\n- $+0.02$ uses the right size but the stretch factor from $x = +1$ instead of $x=-1$. The stretch factor is local to where the input sits.\\n- $+0.0001$ squares the nudge. That gives the value of $f(\\\\Delta x)$, not the change in $f$ near $-1$.\\n- $-0.01$ gets the direction right but leaves out the doubling.\", \"correctAnswer\": \"neg\"}"}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":6,"cacheRead":17510,"cacheWrite":16029,"totalTokens":33547,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":16029},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860994219,"responseId":"msg_011CgMR1Z5HBq5VkbdJ6CTE6"}}

data: {"type":"message_update","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"## What a derivative measures\n\nStart from the definition, read as a statement about nudges:\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(a+\\Delta x) - f(a)}{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nIn words: move the input slightly away from $a$, look at how far the output moves, and divide. As the nudge gets smaller, that ratio settles on one number, and that number is $f'(a)$. Rearranged, the definition says:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> For a small nudge $\\Delta x$ at the point $a$: $\\quad \\Delta f \\approx f'(a)\\,\\Delta x$.\n> The derivative is the **stretch factor** at $a$. It's the number an input nudge gets multiplied by to become the output nudge.\n\nTwo things follow from reading it this way, and both matter later:\n\n- **The stretch factor depends on where you are.** $f(x) = x^2$ stretches nudges by $6$ at $x=3$ and by $1$ at $x = 0.5$. \"The derivative of $f$\" isn't one number. It's a different stretch factor at each input.\n- **The sign tells you the direction.** A stretch factor of $-2$ doubles the nudge and also reverses it. If the input moves right, the output moves left.\n\nThe $\\approx$ means the error shrinks faster than $\\Delta x$ does. Make the nudge 10 times smaller and the error gets much more than 10 times smaller. That's why \"multiply by the stretch factor\" becomes exact in the limit.\n\nThis is the first of the three foundations. Before we stack anything on it, I want to check that the \"stretch factor at a point\" reading feels obvious rather than just familiar. Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field, even if it's one line."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01Xss3nLMjpoEo4ZsrA5Nehs","name":"quiz","arguments":{"type":"check","nodeId":"def-derivative","question":"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?","details":"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge.","options":[{"label":"$+0.02$","value":"pos"},{"label":"$-0.02$","value":"neg"},{"label":"$+0.0001$","value":"sq"},{"label":"$-0.01$","value":"nostretch"}],"explanation":"The stretch factor at $x=-1$ is $f'(-1) = 2\\cdot(-1) = -2$. The nudge gets doubled and reversed:\n$$\\Delta f \\approx f'(-1)\\,\\Delta x = -2 \\times 0.01 = -0.02$$\nYou can check it directly: $(-0.99)^2 = 0.9801$, which is $0.0199$ less than $1$, so about $-0.02$.\n\n- $+0.02$ uses the right size but the stretch factor from $x = +1$ instead of $x=-1$. The stretch factor is local to where the input sits.\n- $+0.0001$ squares the nudge. That gives the value of $f(\\Delta x)$, not the change in $f$ near $-1$.\n- $-0.01$ gets the direction right but leaves out the doubling.","correctAnswer":"neg"}}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":1887,"cacheRead":17510,"cacheWrite":16029,"totalTokens":35428,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":16029,"reasoning":651},"stopReason":"pending","timestamp":1792860994219,"responseId":"msg_011CgMR1Z5HBq5VkbdJ6CTE6"}}

data: {"type":"message_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"## What a derivative measures\n\nStart from the definition, read as a statement about nudges:\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{\\Delta x \\to 0} \\frac{f(a+\\Delta x) - f(a)}{\\Delta x}\n$$\n\nIn words: move the input slightly away from $a$, look at how far the output moves, and divide. As the nudge gets smaller, that ratio settles on one number, and that number is $f'(a)$. Rearranged, the definition says:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> For a small nudge $\\Delta x$ at the point $a$: $\\quad \\Delta f \\approx f'(a)\\,\\Delta x$.\n> The derivative is the **stretch factor** at $a$. It's the number an input nudge gets multiplied by to become the output nudge.\n\nTwo things follow from reading it this way, and both matter later:\n\n- **The stretch factor depends on where you are.** $f(x) = x^2$ stretches nudges by $6$ at $x=3$ and by $1$ at $x = 0.5$. \"The derivative of $f$\" isn't one number. It's a different stretch factor at each input.\n- **The sign tells you the direction.** A stretch factor of $-2$ doubles the nudge and also reverses it. If the input moves right, the output moves left.\n\nThe $\\approx$ means the error shrinks faster than $\\Delta x$ does. Make the nudge 10 times smaller and the error gets much more than 10 times smaller. That's why \"multiply by the stretch factor\" becomes exact in the limit.\n\nThis is the first of the three foundations. Before we stack anything on it, I want to check that the \"stretch factor at a point\" reading feels obvious rather than just familiar. Please write your actual reasoning in the thinking field, even if it's one line."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_01Xss3nLMjpoEo4ZsrA5Nehs","name":"quiz","arguments":{"type":"check","nodeId":"def-derivative","question":"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?","details":"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge.","options":[{"label":"$+0.02$","value":"pos"},{"label":"$-0.02$","value":"neg"},{"label":"$+0.0001$","value":"sq"},{"label":"$-0.01$","value":"nostretch"}],"explanation":"The stretch factor at $x=-1$ is $f'(-1) = 2\\cdot(-1) = -2$. The nudge gets doubled and reversed:\n$$\\Delta f \\approx f'(-1)\\,\\Delta x = -2 \\times 0.01 = -0.02$$\nYou can check it directly: $(-0.99)^2 = 0.9801$, which is $0.0199$ less than $1$, so about $-0.02$.\n\n- $+0.02$ uses the right size but the stretch factor from $x = +1$ instead of $x=-1$. The stretch factor is local to where the input sits.\n- $+0.0001$ squares the nudge. That gives the value of $f(\\Delta x)$, not the change in $f$ near $-1$.\n- $-0.01$ gets the direction right but leaves out the doubling.","correctAnswer":"neg"}}],"api":"claude-code-cli","provider":"claude-code","model":"claude-opus-5-5","usage":{"input":2,"output":1887,"cacheRead":17510,"cacheWrite":16029,"totalTokens":35428,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":16029,"reasoning":651},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792860994219,"responseId":"msg_011CgMR1Z5HBq5VkbdJ6CTE6","rawStopReason":"tool_use","durationMs":29411,"thinkingLevel":"high"}}

data: {"type":"tool_execution_start","toolCallId":"toolu_01Xss3nLMjpoEo4ZsrA5Nehs","toolName":"quiz","args":{"type":"check","nodeId":"def-derivative","question":"Take $f(x) = x^2$. The input sits at $x = -1$ and is nudged by $\\Delta x = +0.01$. About how much does the output change?","details":"No formula for the answer is needed beyond $f'(x) = 2x$. Say in the thinking field what the stretch factor is here and what it does to the nudge.","options":[{"label":"$+0.02$","value":"pos"},{"label":"$-0.02$","value":"neg"},{"label":"$+0.0001$","value":"sq"},{"label":"$-0.01$","value":"nostretch"}],"explanation":"The stretch factor at $x=-1$ is $f'(-1) = 2\\cdot(-1) = -2$. The nudge gets doubled and reversed:\n$$\\Delta f \\approx f'(-1)\\,\\Delta x = -2 \\times 0.01 = -0.02$$\nYou can check it directly: $(-0.99)^2 = 0.9801$, which is $0.0199$ less than $1$, so about $-0.02$.\n\n- $+0.02$ uses the right size but the stretch factor from $x = +1$ instead of $x=-1$. The stretch factor is local to where the input sits.\n- $+0.0001$ squares the nudge. That gives the value of $f(\\Delta x)$, not the change in $f$ near $-1$.\n- $-0.01$ gets the direction right but leaves out the doubling.","correctAnswer":"neg"}}

